---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
const kids = [
  ["Kathleen Georgina", "4 Nov 1870", "29 Feb 1940", "1st daughter", false],
  ["William John", "Nov 1870", "11 May 1951", "m. Fanny Ellen Jefferey", false],
  ["Horace A", "Apr 1872", "18 Jan 1934", "", false],
  ["Isabella Temple “Edie”", "1874", "Dec 1953", "2nd daughter · m. Dalling", false],
  ["Margaret Hellen Walker “Maggie”", "1876", "after 1953", "3rd daughter · m. Bell", false],
  ["Irene “Rene”", "1879", "25 Mar 1969", "4th daughter · a twin · m. Burton", false],
  ["(unknown twin)", "1879", "—", "5th daughter · Irene’s twin; her name is not recorded", false],
  ["Lucy Elizabeth", "4 Aug 1881", "26 May 1957", "6th daughter · m. Sweet", false],
  ["Mary Septima", "17 Sep 1884", "15 Nov 1935", "the SEVENTH daughter · m. Pablo Armando Lerena", true],
  ["Agnes Walker “Aggie”", "3 Aug 1889", "29 Apr 1974", "8th daughter · m. Wood", false],
];
---
<Base title="Mary Septima Taylor"
      eyebrow="Bideford, Northam — and a name that is a count"
      dek="She is buried in the Roman Catholic ground at Maitland under a Spanish surname she took at about twenty. Her own name is a piece of arithmetic, and it turns out to be exactly right.">
<section class="blk">
<h2>The name is a count</h2>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>Mary Septima Taylor</strong> was born
    <strong>17 September 1884</strong>, the ninth of ten children of <strong>William Taylor</strong>
    (1826&ndash;1902) and <strong>Mary Ann Hellen Brayley</strong> (1843&ndash;1900).
  </p>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Child</th><th>Born</th><th>Died</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {kids.map(([n, b, d, note, hi]) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{hi ? <strong>{n}</strong> : n}</td>
            <td class="mono">{b}</td><td class="mono">{d}</td>
            <td class="cap">{hi ? <strong>{note}</strong> : note}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    <em>Septima</em> is Latin for <strong>seventh</strong>, and Victorian parents used such names literally.
    Count the daughters in order of birth &mdash; Kathleen, Isabella, Margaret, Irene, Irene's twin, Lucy
    &mdash; and <strong>Mary Septima is the seventh</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <span class="chip">Inferred</span> Which incidentally settles something the tree leaves blank: the
    unnamed twin of 1879 must have been a <strong>girl</strong>, or the arithmetic does not come out. The
    name her parents gave her ninth child records a sister whose own name has been lost.
  </p>
  <p class="cap">
    Her granddaughter's generation kept it: an infant <strong>Antoinette Septima Lynette Lerena</strong> was
    born on 3 January 1943 and died four days later.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>The line behind her</h2>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> Above William Taylor stands <strong>James Taylor</strong>, born
    about <strong>1796</strong>, and his wife <strong>Harriet Reynolds</strong> (1803&ndash;1827). Harriet
    died on 12 November 1827, when her son William was twenty-one months old. A first
    <strong>William Taylor</strong> had been born on 29 December 1824 and died the following September at
    eight months; the William who lived was given the dead child's name, as families then did.
  </p>
  <p>
    That reaches back <strong>four generations above Mary Septima</strong>, to a man born in the
    1790s &mdash; further than this archive has yet got on the Lerena side, where
    <a href={u("/direct-line")}>Pablo Armando's own parents are still unknown</a>.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>Northam and Bideford, north Devon</h2>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>Mary Septima Taylor was born at
    <strong>Bideford, Devon</strong> on 17 September 1884</strong> — registered there, and christened on
    30 October at <strong>St Margaret's, Northam</strong>, in the <strong>Anglican</strong> church.
  </p>
  <p class="cap">
    Source: <em>England and Wales Birth Registration Index, 1837&ndash;2008</em>, citing the birth
    registration at Bideford, Devon, General Register Office, Southport. Held in the family's own tree.
  </p>
  <p>
    Her father <strong>William Taylor</strong> was born at <strong>Worlingworth in Suffolk</strong> on
    11 February 1826 and came west: he married <strong>Mary Ann Hellen Brayley</strong> at
    <strong>St Mark's, Bideford</strong>, on 26 January 1870, and their children were born and raised
    between <strong>Bideford</strong> and <strong>Northam</strong> — 23 Geneva Place, North Street,
    2 Fore Street. He died on 30 November 1902 at 2 Park Terrace, Northam, of chronic bulbar paralysis, his
    son Horace Archibald present and giving the information; he lies in Northam churchyard, old grave
    XB28&ndash;K8.
  </p>
  <div class="panel" style="margin:1.2rem 0">
    <p style="margin:0">
      <strong>Brayley is a north Devon name</strong>, from Bray. This archive inferred a Devon origin from
      that surname alone, before any place had been seen written down. The family's own records put them at
      <strong>Bideford and Northam</strong> — six miles apart, and both in Bray's country. The inference
      held.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3>A coachman's daughter</h3>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> William Taylor's trade is given as
    <strong>coachman</strong> — with domestic servant, and pensioner at the last.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the horses are on <em>both</em> sides of this marriage. A coachman's daughter of Northam married an
    Argentine <strong>horse-dealer</strong> at the Cape. <a href={u("/horses")}>The horses &rarr;</a>
  </p>

  <h3>A scholar</h3>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> On <strong>1 December 1900</strong> — six weeks after her
    mother's death, at sixteen — she was awarded the <strong>Bishop Phillpotts' Prayer Book Prize</strong>
    by the Exeter Diocesan Board of Education, in the general examination in religious knowledge for
    <strong>monitors, candidates and pupil teachers</strong>: Class III, monitors over sixteen and pupil
    teachers, <strong>one of forty-three out of eighty-eight who passed</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    She was a <strong>pupil teacher</strong> — a girl of sixteen already teaching, and being examined on
    it. She signed her own name in a firm hand at her wedding eleven years later.
  </p>
  <p class="cap">
    In the 1891 census, aged six, she was at <strong>2 Crystal Villas, Stanton Road, Barnes, Surrey</strong>
    — living with an aunt, away from Devon.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>The ship</h2>
  <p>
    <span class="chip lore">Family lore</span> The story is that Mary Septima and Pablo Armando
    <strong>met aboard a ship bound for South Africa</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The objection is a fair one and was raised by the family itself: <em>she</em> was in England, but
    <em>he</em> was an Argentine going from Argentina to South Africa &mdash; so what was he doing on a ship
    out of England? Three things make the story work better than it first looks.
  </p>

  <h3>1. The River Plate reached the Cape through Europe</h3>
  <p>
    In the 1900s there was no dense direct passenger service between Buenos Aires and Cape Town. The
    ordinary way to make that journey was to sail to <strong>Europe</strong> and change ships &mdash;
    Southampton being the hub of the mail service to the Cape. On that reading he was never "in England" in
    any meaningful sense: <strong>he changed ships there</strong>, and the leg on which they met was the
    Southampton&ndash;Cape Town run. "He came from Argentina" and "they met on a ship from England" are not
    in conflict at all. <span class="chip">Inferred</span>
  </p>

  <h3>2. The horses were already moving that way</h3>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> The South African War of 1899&ndash;1902 consumed horses on a
    scale never seen before: of some 519,000 horses used, <strong>about 360,000 were shipped into South
    Africa</strong>, drawn from England, Australia, Canada, New Zealand, Burma &mdash; and
    <strong>Argentina</strong>. Contemporary British officers grumbled about the "mongrel Argentines" and
    set Australian bushmen to breaking them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Pablo Armando Lerena was born in February 1882. He was <strong>seventeen when that war began and twenty
    when it ended</strong> &mdash; and he was a horseman, later an Argentine cavalryman and a Cape Town
    trainer. A young man of exactly his age and trade moving from the River Plate to the Cape in those years
    was moving <em>with</em> a trade, not against the current. <a href={u("/horses")}>The horses &rarr;</a>
  </p>

  <h3>3. She had a documented reason to be aboard</h3>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> Her mother died in <strong>April 1900</strong> and her father
    in <strong>November 1902</strong>. She was fifteen and then eighteen.
  </p>
  <p>
    And from <strong>1901</strong> the Colonial Office and the emigration societies ran an organised
    programme to send <strong>single British women to South Africa</strong> &mdash; a scheme the war had
    turned from charity into imperial policy, aimed at securing the new colonies by settling British women
    in them. An orphaned Englishwoman of nineteen or twenty sailing for the Cape between 1903 and 1905 is
    not an anomaly. <strong>She is the type the scheme existed to carry.</strong>
  </p>

  <h3>What would prove it</h3>
  <p>
    All three of those are context, not evidence about these two people. The story is testable, and by a
    single class of record:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <strong>UK outward passenger lists (BT 27)</strong> survive from 1890 and are indexed by name. If
    Mary Septima Taylor and Pablo Armando Lerena sailed from Southampton for the Cape between about 1903
    and 1905, <strong>both their names are on one list</strong>.
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Their first child <a href={u("/estate")}>Roque</a> was born at Cape Town on
    <strong>25 November 1905</strong>, which brackets the crossing: they were both at the Cape, and married,
    by early 1905. <a href={u("/open-questions")}>Open questions &rarr;</a>
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>The marriage &mdash; and it is not where the story expects it</h2>
  <div class="panel" style="margin:1.4rem 0">
    <p style="margin-top:0"><span class="chip doc">Documented</span></p>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">
      <strong>Cape Province marriage register no. 160, 3 September 1911</strong>, solemnized at
      <strong>Wynberg</strong>: <em>"Married in <strong>St Dominic's</strong> at Wynberg aforesaid, after
      <strong>banns</strong>."</em> Images supplied by the National Archives of South Africa.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th style="width:12rem">Groom</th><td><strong>Pablo Armando Lerena</strong>, 29, European,
          <strong>bachelor</strong>, <strong>horse-dealer</strong>, of <strong>Diep River</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Bride</th><td><strong>Mary Septima Taylor</strong>, 27, European,
          <strong>spinster</strong>, of <strong>Diep River</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Signed</th><td>Both signed their own names in full, in confident hands</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Examined by</th><td>"Bert&hellip;" — very likely <strong>Bertram W. Glynn</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>They married six years after their first son was born</h3>
  <p>
    Roque was born at Cape Town on <strong>25 November 1905</strong>. His parents married on
    <strong>3 September 1911</strong>, and the register calls them <strong>bachelor</strong> and
    <strong>spinster</strong> — so neither had been married before, to each other or to anyone.
  </p>
  <p>
    <span class="chip">Inferred</span> That closes a puzzle this archive had already noticed. Roque's
    baptism was delayed six years, to <strong>12 June 1911</strong> — and it was performed by
    <strong>Bertram W. Glynn</strong>, whose name then appears examining his parents' marriage register
    three months later. <strong>The family was brought into the church in the course of a single year:
    the child in June, the parents in September.</strong> Roque was born outside marriage and legitimated
    by it.
  </p>
  <p class="cap">
    Set down plainly, because this archive sets things down plainly. It is also, on the record, a couple who
    stayed together until her death and a man who never remarried — a widower still on his passport in 1940
    and on his death certificate in 1950.
  </p>
  <h3>She changed her church</h3>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> On <strong>16 December 1911</strong>, three months after the
    wedding, <strong>Mary Septima was herself baptised a Catholic</strong> at Corpus Christi, Wynberg.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the year runs: the <strong>son</strong> baptised in June, the <strong>parents married</strong> in
    September, the <strong>mother received</strong> in December. An Anglican girl from a Devon vicarage
    parish, christened at St Margaret's Northam in 1884, came into her Argentine husband's church at
    twenty-seven — and was buried from it, in the Roman Catholic ground at Maitland, twenty-four years
    later.
  </p>

  <h3>And it changes how the ship must be searched</h3>
  <p>
    The marriage does not disprove the crossing; they could well have met in 1904 or 1905. But it removes an
    assumption. <strong>She was Mary Septima <em>Taylor</em> until September 1911</strong>, so on any
    passenger list before that date she appears under <strong>Taylor</strong>, never Lerena — and, being
    unmarried, the two may have travelled <strong>separately</strong> and will not share a berth entry.
  </p>
  <p class="cap">
    It also gives his trade at the earliest date yet: <strong>horse-dealer</strong>. The sequence runs
    horse-dealer (1911) &rarr; trainer (1940, and a yard taking apprentices in the 1940s) &rarr; race horse
    owner (1950). <a href={u("/horses")}>The horses &rarr;</a>
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>Her end</h2>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> She died on <strong>15 November 1935</strong> at
    <strong>34 Florence Villas, Observatory</strong>, aged 51, of <strong>carcinoma of the uterus and
    bladder with septic nephritis</strong>, and was buried the next day in the <strong>Roman Catholic
    allotment of Maitland (Woltemade) Cemetery no. 1, plot 7719 A</strong>, six feet nine inches down,
    Father Graham officiating. The doctor who certified her death, <strong>J. R. E. Lee</strong>, would
    sign her husband's certificate fifteen years later.
  </p>
  <div class="panel" style="margin:1.2rem 0">
    <p style="margin-top:0"><span class="chip lore">Family memory, recorded by David Defranceski</span></p>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">
      <em>"She had a difficult time, especially when she got ill, with Luis being so challenging and at odds
      with his father Pablo… She was very soft with her sons and loved all three dearly; it broke her heart
      when Pablo cut Luis off from the family and home."</em>
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    <span class="chip">Inferred</span> That is not only a memory. It is <strong>legible in the
    will</strong>. When Pablo Armando died in 1950 he left <strong>Luis half a share</strong> — the same as
    <strong>Rose Lilian Theys, "no relation"</strong> — while Ricardo and Nuno held three-quarters of the
    estate between them. The estrangement his mother grieved over is written into the arithmetic of his
    father's estate. <a href={u("/estate")}>The estate &rarr;</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    She left three grown sons &mdash; Roque, Ricardo and Nuno &mdash; and her husband did not remarry.
    He was still described as a widower on his Argentine passport in 1940 and on his death certificate in
    1950. <a href={u("/estate")}>The estate &rarr;</a>
  </p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The Taylors had horses too</h2>
  <p class="dek">1891 and 1901 censuses of Northam, Devon.</p>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:16px">
    <p>This archive carried William Taylor — Mary Septima&rsquo;s father — as a <em>coachman</em>. The
      censuses say something sharper. In <strong>1891</strong>, aged 65, the enumerator writes his
      occupation as:</p>
    <p style="margin:18px 0"><strong style="font-size:1.1em">&ldquo;Cab driver retired sergt p cavaloy&rdquo;</strong>
      <span class="cap">— cab driver; retired sergeant, cavalry</span></p>
    <p>By <strong>1901</strong> he is a widower of North Street, Northam, and simply an
      <strong>army pensioner</strong>. His eldest son drove a cab beside him; his second son was a footman.</p>
    <p><strong>So the woman Pablo Armando married came from horses as well.</strong> The family story about
      him is entirely a horse story — trainer, breeder, stables on the Koeberg Road, four generations of
      jockeys after him. It turns out her father spent his working life in the saddle and on the box: a
      cavalry sergeant, then a cab driver in a small Devon town.</p>
    <p>That is not evidence of anything, and it does not explain how they met. But this archive has spent a
      long time treating Mary Septima as the ordinary English half of the story, and that was plainly
      wrong. <strong>Horses stand behind both sides of this marriage.</strong></p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:22px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>1901 — North Street, Northam</th><th>Age</th><th>Occupation</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>William Taylor</strong>, head, widower</td><td class="m">75</td><td>Army pensioner</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Kathleen, daughter</td><td class="m">30</td><td>—</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Margaret, daughter</td><td class="m">24</td><td>—</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Lucy, daughter</td><td class="m">18</td><td>—</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Mary, daughter</strong></td><td class="m">16</td><td><strong>School teacher</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Agnes, daughter</td><td class="m">11</td><td>—</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px"><strong>An absence, recorded rather than explained.</strong>
    Mary Septima is <strong>not in the 1891 census</strong>. Born September 1884, she should be a
    six-year-old in that household between Lucy and Agnes. She is not there, and a search of the whole of
    Devon for a Mary Taylor born 1883&ndash;85 returns four girls, none of them her. She was probably away
    from home that night, or the index has mangled her — either is likelier than anything dramatic. But it
    is an absence, and it should be checked against the original image.</div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:16px">Her mother, <strong>Mary Ann Hellen</strong>, is recorded in 1891
    as born in <strong>London, Middlesex</strong> — though Brayley, her maiden name, is a north Devon
    surname. Also worth resolving.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What she was doing at sixteen</h2>
  <p class="dek">Western Times, 4 April 1901, page 7 — repeated in the North Devon Herald a week later.</p>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:16px">
    <p>The Exeter Diocesan Board of Education examined the pupil teachers and monitors of Devon&rsquo;s
      Church schools each year, and printed the results by name, age and parish. In April 1901, under
      <strong>Set II — monitors over sixteen, and pupil teachers in their first year</strong>, eighty-eight
      candidates were examined. In Class III, between a boy from Honiton and a girl from Honiton, sits:</p>
    <p style="margin:18px 0 18px 0"><strong style="font-size:1.15em">&ldquo;Mary Septima Taylor, 16, Northam&rdquo;</strong></p>
    <p><strong>She was training to be a schoolteacher.</strong> A pupil teacher stayed on at her own village
      school and learned the work in it, examined once a year, paid a little. It is the first thing this
      archive knows about her that is not a date.</p>
    <p>And it sharpens the shape of her life to something quite startling. <strong>A pupil teacher at
      Northam in April 1901. &ldquo;Mrs Lerena&rdquo;, crossing from Cape Town to Southampton with a small
      son, by February 1909.</strong> Eight years, and the whole distance between a north Devon parish
      school and an Argentine horseman&rsquo;s household at the Cape. What happened in those eight years is
      the largest thing still missing from her story.</p>
    <p class="cap">The newspaper OCR is poor and the lists run together. Her own entry reads unambiguously
      in both papers; the class boundaries around it were reconstructed from a damaged column and should be
      re-checked against the image before anything is built on them.</p>
  </div>
</section>


<section class="blk">
  <h2>The eight-year hole — and two ships that might close it</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>A pupil teacher at Northam in April 1901.</strong> <strong>&ldquo;Mrs M T Lerena&rdquo;,
      crossing from Cape Town to Southampton with a three-year-old son, in February 1909.</strong> Between
      those two facts lie eight years and six thousand miles, and they are, I think, the largest
      <em>answerable</em> gap left in this family&rsquo;s story — larger than where Pablo Armando was born,
      and sitting in records that survive and are indexed.</p>
    <p>The outward passenger lists give a clean negative first: <strong>no Mary Taylor of her age left
      Britain for South Africa at all</strong>, in the whole range 1890&ndash;1960. But the index frequently
      carries <em>no forename</em>. Drop it, and filter on surname, age and destination, and two candidates
      appear in exactly the right window:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:20px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Passenger</th><th>Sailed</th><th>Ship</th><th>Reference</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Miss Taylor</strong>, 19, single, <strong>Domestic</strong></td>
          <td class="m">Southampton &rarr; Cape<br><strong>9 September 1903</strong></td>
          <td><em>Harlech Castle</em><br><span class="cap">Donald Currie &amp; Co, 254 passengers</span></td>
          <td class="m">TNA BT 27/0420, p.68</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Miss Taylor</strong>, 19, single</td>
          <td class="m">Southampton &rarr; Cape<br><strong>9 April 1904</strong></td>
          <td><em>Walmer Castle</em><br><span class="cap">Union-Castle, 271 passengers</span></td>
          <td class="m">TNA BT 27/0448</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px"><strong>Mary Septima was born on 17 September 1884.</strong> On
    <strong>9 September 1903 she was eight days short of her nineteenth birthday</strong> — and the manifest
    records the passenger as nineteen. The occupation fits the life rather than the legend: a pupil teacher
    at sixteen, in a cab driver&rsquo;s household of ten children, at a moment when the Cape was
    <strong>actively recruiting British domestic servants</strong> on assisted passage. A north Devon girl
    going out as a domestic in 1903 is not a romantic story — it is the ordinary one, and it would put her
    in Cape Town about two years before Roque was born there.</div>
  <p class="prose" style="margin-top:20px"><span class="chip inf">A candidate, not an identification</span>
    <strong>Taylor is among the commonest surnames in England</strong>, and &ldquo;Miss Taylor, 19&rdquo;
    will have sailed for the Cape many times in those years. Two records fitting a window is not proof.
    <strong>The original manifests would show the forename</strong> — the index does not carry it, but the
    page usually does. Both references are given above.</p>
</section>

</Base>
